        ['id' => 'areas', 'h2' => 'What each area means and how to improve it', 'html' => <<<'HTML'
<h3>Growth</h3>
<p>Paul Graham's essay "Startup = Growth" argues that growth is the one thing that defines a startup, and suggests measuring it as a weekly rate, ideally of revenue, or of active users if you don't charge yet. Among Y Combinator companies he described 5&ndash;7% a week as a good rate and 1% as a sign the founders hadn't worked out what they were doing yet. The quiz asks about monthly growth because most founders track it that way. To improve it, pick one number, set a target, and review it every week.</p>
<h3>Revenue and stage</h3>
<p>Charging early tells you whether the problem is painful enough to pay for. If you have users but no revenue, test a price with a small group this month, even a modest one.</p>
<h3>Retention</h3>
<p>Retention shows whether people keep finding value. In Y Combinator's startup library lesson on cohort retention, partner David Lieb explains how to group users into cohorts by sign-up period, decide what counts as an active user and pick a time window that suits how often the product should be used. A16z's "16 Startup Metrics" makes a similar point: define "active" explicitly, track cohorts, and be honest about churn. If you answered "Not sure", start by measuring it; the quiz rewards knowing.</p>
<h3>Team</h3>
<p>Sam Altman's Startup Playbook says strong founders matter most and that a founding team needs someone who can build and someone who can sell and talk to users. Part-time solo founders score lower here because progress is slower, not because one person can't start a company.</p>
<h3>Distribution</h3>
<p>In "Do Things that Don't Scale", Paul Graham writes that most startups have to recruit their first users by hand. Word of mouth is a good sign, but one repeatable channel is what lets you grow on purpose. Ask your last 20 customers how they found you.</p>
<h3>Funding and runway</h3>
<p>A16z's metrics guide suggests watching net burn (spending minus revenue) to see how long cash will last. Twelve months of runway gives you time to find growth without raising in a hurry. You can follow recent rounds on our <a href="/funding-tracker/">funding tracker</a>.</p>
<h3>Moat, market and speed</h3>
<p>A moat is anything that gets stronger as you grow: data, network effects, switching costs. Market size sets the ceiling; size it bottom-up from the number of buyers and what each would pay. Shipping speed is the engine that improves everything else, and weekly releases users can see are a good default.</p>
HTML
